.TH CI_DNS_RECORD 3 "12 November 2023"
.SH NAME
ci_dns_class_t, ci_dns_flags_t, ci_dns_opcode_t, ci_dns_parse,
ci_dns_rcode_t, ci_dns_record_create, ci_dns_record_destroy,
ci_dns_record_get_flags, ci_dns_record_get_id, ci_dns_record_get_opcode,
ci_dns_record_get_rcode, ci_dns_record_query_add, ci_dns_record_query_cnt,
ci_dns_record_query_get, ci_dns_rec_type_t, ci_dns_write \-
DNS Record parsing, writing, creating and destroying functions.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ci.h>

void ci_dns_record_destroy(ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec);

ci_status_t ci_dns_parse(const unsigned char *buf,
                             size_t buf_len, unsigned int flags,
                             ci_dns_record_t **dnsrec);

ci_status_t ci_dns_write(const ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec,
                             unsigned char **buf, size_t *buf_len);

ci_status_t ci_dns_record_create(ci_dns_record_t **dnsrec,
                                     unsigned short      id,
                                     unsigned short      flags,
                                     ci_dns_opcode_t   opcode,
                                     ci_dns_rcode_t    rcode);

ci_dns_record_t *ci_dns_record_duplicate(const ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec);

unsigned short ci_dns_record_get_id(const ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec);

ci_bool_t ci_dns_record_set_id(ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec,
                                   unsigned short id);

unsigned short ci_dns_record_get_flags(const ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec);

ci_dns_opcode_t ci_dns_record_get_opcode(const ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec);

ci_dns_rcode_t ci_dns_record_get_rcode(const ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec);

ci_status_t ci_dns_record_query_add(ci_dns_record_t  *dnsrec,
                                        const char         *name,
                                        ci_dns_rec_type_t qtype,
                                        ci_dns_class_t    qclass);

ci_status_t ci_dns_record_query_set_name(ci_dns_record_t  *dnsrec,
                                             size_t              idx,
                                             const char         *name);

ci_status_t ci_dns_record_query_set_type(ci_dns_record_t  *dnsrec,
                                             size_t              idx,
                                             ci_dns_rec_type_t qtype);

size_t ci_dns_record_query_cnt(const ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec);

ci_status_t ci_dns_record_query_get(const ci_dns_record_t *dnsrec,
                                        size_t idx, const char **name,
                                        ci_dns_rec_type_t *qtype,
                                        ci_dns_class_t *qclass);

.fi
.SH ENUMERATIONS

.B ci_dns_rec_type_t -
DNS Record types handled by c-ci.  Some record types may only be valid
on requests, and some may only be valid on responses:
.RS 4
.B CI_REC_TYPE_A
- Host address
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_NS
- Authoritative server
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_CNAME
- Canonical name
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_SOA
- Start of authority zone
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_PTR
- Domain name pointer
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_HINFO
- Host information
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_MX
- Mail routing information
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_TXT
- Text strings
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_SIG
- RFC 2535. RFC 2931. SIG Record
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_AAAA
- RFC 3596. Ip6 Address
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_SRV
- RFC 2782. Server Selection
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_NAPTR
- RFC 3403. Naming Authority Pointer
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_OPT
- RFC 6891. EDNS0 option (meta-RR). Pseudo Record.
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_TLSA
- RFC 6698. DNS-Based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) Transport Layer Security (TLS) Protocol: TLSA
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_SVCB
- RFC 9460. General Purpose Service Binding
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_HTTPS -
- RFC 9460. Service Binding type for use with HTTPS
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_ANY
- Wildcard match.  Not response RR
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_URI
- RFC 7553. Uniform Resource Identifier
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_CAA
- RFC 6844. Certification Authority Authorization
.br
.B CI_REC_TYPE_RAW_RR
- Used as an indicator that the RR record is not parsed, but provided in wire
format
.br
.RE

.B ci_dns_class_t -
DNS Classes for requests and responses:
.RS 4
.B CI_CLASS_IN
- Internet
.br
.B CI_CLASS_CHAOS
- CHAOS
.br
.B CI_CLASS_HESOID
- Hesoid [Dyer 87]
.br
.B CI_CLASS_NONE
- RFC 2136
.br
.B CI_CLASS_ANY
- Any class (requests only)
.br
.RE

.B ci_dns_opcode_t -
DNS Header Opcodes:
.RS 4
.B CI_OPCODE_QUERY
- Standard query
.br
.B CI_OPCODE_IQUERY
- Inverse query. Obsolete
.br
.B CI_OPCODE_STATUS
- Name server status query
.br
.B CI_OPCODE_NOTIFY
- Zone change notification (RFC 1996)
.br
.B CI_OPCODE_UPDATE
- Zone update message (RFC 2136)
.br
.RE

.B ci_dns_flags_t -
DNS Header Flags:
.RS 4
.B CI_FLAG_QR
- QR. If set, is a response
.br
.B CI_FLAG_AA
- Authoritative Answer. If set, is authoritative
.br
.B CI_FLAG_TC
- Truncation. If set, is truncated response
.br
.B CI_FLAG_RD
- Recursion Desired. If set, recursion is desired
.br
.B CI_FLAG_RA
- Recursion Available. If set, server supports recursion
.br
.B CI_FLAG_AD
- RFC 2065. Authentic Data bit indicates in a response that the data included
has been verified by the server providing it
.br
.B CI_FLAG_CD
- RFC 2065. Checking Disabled bit indicates in a query that non-verified data
is acceptable to the resolver sending the query
.br
.RE

.B ci_dns_rcode_t -
DNS Response codes from server:
.RS 4
.B CI_RCODE_NOERROR
- Success
.br
.B CI_RCODE_FORMERR
- Format error. The name server was unable to interpret the query
.br
.B CI_RCODE_SERVFAIL
- Server Failure. The name server was unable to process this query due to a
problem with the nameserver
.br
.B CI_RCODE_NXDOMAIN
- Name Error.  Meaningful only for responses from an authoritative name server,
this code signifies that the domain name referenced in the query does not exist.
.br
.B CI_RCODE_NOTIMP
- Not implemented.  The name server does not support the requested kind of query
.br
.B CI_RCODE_REFUSED
- Refused. The name server refuses to perform the specified operation for policy
reasons.
.br
.B CI_RCODE_YXDOMAIN
- RFC 2136. Some name that ought not to exist, does exist
.br
.B CI_RCODE_YXRRSET
- RFC 2136. Some RRset that ought to not exist, does exist
.br
.B CI_RCODE_NXRRSET
- RFC 2136. Some RRset that ought to exist, does not exist
.br
.B CI_RCODE_NOTAUTH
- RFC 2136. The server is not authoritative for the zone named in the Zone section.
.br
.B CI_RCODE_NOTZONE
- RFC 2136. A name used in the Prerequisite or Update Section is not within the
zone denoted by the Zone Section.
.br
.B CI_RCODE_DSOTYPEI
- RFC 8409. DSO-TYPE Not implemented
.br
.B CI_RCODE_BADSIG
- RFC 8945. TSIG Signature Failure
.br
.B CI_RCODE_BADKEY
- RFC 8945. Key not recognized
.br
.B CI_RCODE_BADTIME
- RFC 8945. Signature out of time window
.br
.B CI_RCODE_BADMODE
- RFC 2930. Bad TKEY Mode
.br
.B CI_RCODE_BADNAME
- RFC 2930. Duplicate Key Name
.br
.B CI_RCODE_BADALG
- RFC 2930. Algorithm not supported
.br
.B CI_RCODE_BADTRUNC
- RFC 8945. Bad Truncation
.br
.B CI_RCODE_BADCOOKIE
- RFC 7973. Bad/missing Server Cookie
.br
.RE

.B ci_dns_parse_flags_t -
Flags for altering \fIci_dns_parse(3)\fP behaviour:
.RS 4
.B CI_DNS_PARSE_AN_BASE_RAW
- Parse Answer Section from RFC 1035 that allow name compression as RAW RR type
.br
.B CI_DNS_PARSE_NS_BASE_RAW
- Parse Authority Section from RFC 1035 that allow name compression as RAW RR type
.br
.B CI_DNS_PARSE_AR_BASE_RAW
- Parse Additional Section from RFC 1035 that allow name compression as RAW RR type
.br
.B CI_DNS_PARSE_AN_EXT_RAW
- Parse Answer Section from later RFCs (no name compression) as RAW RR type
.br
.B CI_DNS_PARSE_NS_EXT_RAW
- Parse Authority Section from later RFCs (no name compression) as RAW RR type
.br
.B CI_DNS_PARSE_AR_EXT_RAW
- Parse Additional Section from later RFCs (no name compression) as RAW RR type
.br
.RE

.SH DESCRIPTION

The \fIci_dns_record_destroy(3)\fP function destroys the memory associated
with the dns record created by either \fIci_dns_record_create(3)\fP or
\fIci_dns_parse(3)\fP passed in via
.IR dnsrec .

The \fIci_dns_parse(3)\fP function parses the buffer provided in
.IR buf
with length provided in
.IR buf_len.
The
.IR flags
parameter can be one or more \fIci_dns_parse_flags_t\fP, or zero if no
flags are needed.  The resulting dns record data structure is stored into the
variable pointed to by
.IR dnsrec
and must be destroyed using \fIci_dns_record_destroy(3)\fP.

The \fIci_dns_write(3)\fP function takes a populated DNS record structure in
.IR dnsrec
and writes a wire-format DNS message into the variable pointed to by
.IR buf
and writes the length of the buffer into the variable pointed to by
.IR buf_len.
The buffer must be destroyed using \fIci_free_string(3)\fP.

The \fIci_dns_record_create(3)\fP function creates an empty DNS record structure
in the variable pointed to by
.IR dnsrec.
The
.IR id
parameter is the DNS message id, however if passing to \fIci_send(3)\fP this
identifier will be overwritten, so should typically be 0. The
.IR flags
parameter is one or more \fIci_dns_flags_t\fP.  The opcode is passed in the
.IR opcode
parameter and should typically be \fICI_OPCODE_QUERY\fP.  The response code
is meant mostly for responses and is passed in the
.IR rcode
parameter and is typically \fPCI_RCODE_NOERROR\fP.

The \fIci_dns_record_duplicate(3)\fP function duplicates an existing DNS
record structure.  This may be useful if needing to save a result as retrieved
from \fIci_send_dnsrec(3)\fP or \fIci_search_dnsrec(3)\fP.  The structure
to be duplicated is passed in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter, and the duplicated copy is returned, or NULL on error such as
out of memory.

The \fIci_dns_record_get_id(3)\fP function is used to retrieve the DNS
message id from the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.

The \fIci_dns_record_set_id(3)\fP function is used to set the DNS
message id in the
.IR id
parameter from the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.  This id will be overwritten when passing the record to c-ci,
so mostly exists for external purposes.

The \fIci_dns_record_get_flags(3)\fP function is used to retrieve the DNS
message flags from the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.

The \fIci_dns_record_get_opcode(3)\fP function is used to retrieve the DNS
message flags from the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.

The \fIci_dns_record_get_rcode(3)\fP function is used to retrieve the DNS
message response code from the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.


The \fIci_dns_record_query_add(3)\fP function is used to add a question to
the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.  The domain name specified for the question is provided in the
.IR name
parameter, along with the question type in the
.IR qtype
parameter and the question class (typically \fICI_CLASS_IN\fP) in the
.IR qclass
parameter.

The \fIci_dns_record_query_set_name(3)\fP function is used to modify the
question name in the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.  The index of the query, which must be less than
\fIci_dns_record_query_cnt(3)\fP, is provided in the
.IR idx
parameter. The new domain name is provided in the
.IR name
parameter. Care should be taken as this will cause invalidation of any
\fIname\fP pointer retrieved from \fIci_dns_Record_query_get(3)\fP.  This
function is useful if sending multiple similar queries without re-creating
the entire DNS query.

The \fIci_dns_record_query_set_type(3)\fP function is used to modify the
question type in the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.  The index of the query, which must be less than
\fIci_dns_record_query_cnt(3)\fP, is provided in the
.IR idx
parameter. The new query type is provided in the
.IR qtype
parameter.

The \fIci_dns_record_query_cnt(3)\fP function is used to retrieve the number
of DNS questions in the DNS record provided in the
.IR dnsrec
parameter.

The \fIci_dns_record_query_get(3)\fP function is used to retrieve the details
of a single DNS question in the provided
.IR dnsrec
parameter.  The index provided in the
.IR idx
parameter must be less than the value returned from \fIci_dns_record_query_cnt(3)\fP.
The DNS question name will be returned in the variable pointed to by the
.IR name
parameter, this may be provided as NULL if the name is not needed.  This pointer
will be invalided by any call to \fIci_dns_record_query_set_name(3)\fP.
The DNS question type will be returned in the variable pointed to by the
.IR qtype
parameter, this may be provided as NULL if the type is not needed.
The DNS question class will be returned in the variable pointed to by the
.IR qclass
parameter, this may be provided as NULL if the class is not needed.


.SH RETURN VALUES

\fIci_dns_parse(3)\fP, \fIci_dns_write(3)\fP, \fIci_dns_record_create(3)\fP,
\fIci_dns_record_query_add(3)\fP, and \fIci_dns_record_query_get(3)\fP all
return an \fIci_status_t\fP error code.
.B CI_SUCCESS
is returned on success,
.B CI_ENOMEM
is returned on out of memory,
.B CI_EFORMERR
is returned on misuse.

\fIci_dns_record_get_id(3)\fP, \fIci_dns_record_set_id(3)\fP,
\fIci_dns_record_get_flags(3)\fP, \fIci_dns_record_get_opcode(3)\fP,
\fIci_dns_record_get_rcode(3)\fP, and \fIci_dns_record_query_cnt(3)\fP
all returned their prescribed datatype values and in general can't fail except
for misuse cases, in which a 0 may be returned, however 0 can also be a valid
return value for most of these functions.


.SH AVAILABILITY
These functions were first introduced in c-ci version 1.22.0.
